00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:31.840
And the wide variety
of VM families

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:33.560
helps you get the
tailored support

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:35.340
for your diverse
computing needs.

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:40.770
Our VMs are designed
with all that in mind.

00:03:40.770 --> 00:03:43.190
They are designed for
real world scenarios

00:03:43.190 --> 00:03:46.070
so that you can run the
workloads you want in Google

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.520
Cloud and take advantage
of an optimized computing

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:52.400
infrastructure that provides
you with the right capabilities

00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:55.610
for all your application
needs and helps you accelerate

00:03:55.610 --> 00:03:57.788
your cloud transformation.

00:03:57.788 --> 00:03:59.330
This can help you
get your scientists

00:03:59.330 --> 00:04:02.420
in front of innovation, and
your data analysts in front

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:05.900
of insights, and your
mission critical applications

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:07.325
in front of your works force.

00:04:12.390 --> 00:04:15.750
We do that by offering
virtual machine types that

00:04:15.750 --> 00:04:18.870
fit the different types of
workloads you might be running.

00:04:18.870 --> 00:04:22.290
Across our offering, we
focus on three key pillars

00:04:22.290 --> 00:04:25.620
that you will see
me covering today.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:28.610
The first one is that our
virtual machines will always

00:04:28.610 --> 00:04:31.070
offer you leading
price performance

00:04:31.070 --> 00:04:33.620
to ensure that you are running
your applications effectively

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.620
and efficiently, and that you
are seeing improvements year

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:38.000
over year.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:41.570
We do that by leveraging
innovations across CPU vendors

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:44.690
and also by leveraging
unique Google technologies

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:46.580
that we've used
internally for years

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:49.950
to run our own infrastructure.

00:04:49.950 --> 00:04:52.640
We also realize that your
workloads are not equal.

00:04:52.640 --> 00:04:55.220
Some of them optimize
for high CPU performance,

00:04:55.220 --> 00:04:57.680
while others focus
on high-end memory

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:00.740
configurations for business
critical databases.

00:05:00.740 --> 00:05:03.650
You will see that our
offerings are optimized

00:05:03.650 --> 00:05:05.750
for these different
requirements,

00:05:05.750 --> 00:05:09.730
and they prioritize the needs
of the different applications.

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:12.420
The third pillar is that
we know that you don't

00:05:12.420 --> 00:05:14.750
turn on just a virtual machine.

00:05:14.750 --> 00:05:16.840
It's all about the
end-to-end experience

00:05:16.840 --> 00:05:20.720
and the end-to-end capabilities
across networking, storage,

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.810
and compute, and
other capabilities

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:25.580
that make up your
infrastructure experience.

00:05:25.580 --> 00:05:27.940
And because of
that, the offerings

00:05:27.940 --> 00:05:30.900
we'll be discussing today
really cover the spectrum

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:33.400
and make sure that you can get
all the capabilities that you

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:33.900
need.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:41.470
Our VM families, our
virtual machine types,

00:05:41.470 --> 00:05:45.770
come in several shapes,
from general purpose

00:05:45.770 --> 00:05:49.160
to workload optimized.

00:05:49.160 --> 00:05:52.290
Starting on this
chart from the left,

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:54.960
you'll see that our general
purpose VMs are simple,

00:05:54.960 --> 00:05:59.150
flexible, and the best fit for
the majority of your workloads.

00:05:59.150 --> 00:06:02.470
E2 are our general
purpose VMs that

00:06:02.470 --> 00:06:04.630
allow you to benefit
from deep savings,

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:08.980
up to 30% of our
general purpose pricing.

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.110
This family of VMs is
great for customers

00:06:11.110 --> 00:06:14.140
who do not have application
dependencies on a specific CPU

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:15.190
architecture.

00:06:15.190 --> 00:06:18.250
You simply pick that amount of
VCQ and memory that you want,

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:20.080
and we'll provision it for you.

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.800
We proactively adjust the
capacity for the entire fleet

00:06:23.800 --> 00:06:25.870
to optimize
utilization, and that

00:06:25.870 --> 00:06:29.740
means that we give you whichever
processor family is available.

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:32.080
Based on that, we
enable you to save a lot

00:06:32.080 --> 00:06:35.310
by leveraging E2 VMs.

00:06:35.310 --> 00:06:37.815
The second VM type that
we'll talk about today

00:06:37.815 --> 00:06:42.570
is the family of general
purpose VMs, N2 and N2D.

00:06:42.570 --> 00:06:45.600
These are our most
flexible VM types.

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.190
They offer leading
performance per dollar,

00:06:48.190 --> 00:06:52.170
the latest CPU architectures
from both Intel and AMD,

00:06:52.170 --> 00:06:55.530
and a wide range of
shapes and capabilities.

00:06:55.530 --> 00:06:59.870
You can simply attach a lot of
other preferences to these VMs.

00:06:59.870 --> 00:07:02.250
N2 and N2D are the
next generation

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:07.650
following N1 VMs, offering a
significant performance jump.

00:07:07.650 --> 00:07:10.980
Now let's move to the
workload-optimized VMs.

00:07:10.980 --> 00:07:14.910
Compute-optimized VMs
are the best fit VM type

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:18.840
for compute-intensive
workloads, including AAA gaming,

00:07:18.840 --> 00:07:22.680
electronic design automation,
and high performance computing.

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.890
They are powered by
high-frequency Intel scalable

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:30.300
CPUs and offer approximately
40% higher performance compared

00:07:30.300 --> 00:07:31.320
to other GC VMs.

00:07:34.040 --> 00:07:38.730
Next on this list are
our memory-optimized VMs.

00:07:38.730 --> 00:07:41.800
They are the best fit for
memory-intensive workloads,

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:44.020
including in-memory
databases like SAP

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.490
HANA, in-memory analytics,
and in-memory cache.

00:07:47.490 --> 00:07:50.430
Memory optimized VMs offer the
highest memory configuration

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:52.770
available on GCP,
and they can be

00:07:52.770 --> 00:07:56.990
configured with up to 12
terabytes of memory today.

00:07:56.990 --> 00:08:01.790
Finally, we're working on a
new GPU optimize VM type, A2,

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:04.220
that will offer the
latest GPUs available

00:08:04.220 --> 00:08:05.870
and will be available
later this year.

00:08:08.770 --> 00:08:11.790
Now let's start going into
these different VM families,

00:08:11.790 --> 00:08:14.460
and let us understand how they
fit your different application

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:18.110
requirements.

00:08:18.110 --> 00:08:20.330
We'll start with the
general purpose VMs,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.190
and we'll start with E2.

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:26.380
For general purpose workloads
that prioritize cost savings,

00:08:26.380 --> 00:08:30.990
E2 VMs offer up to 31%
savings compared to N1 VMs,

00:08:30.990 --> 00:08:34.350
on both lists and
commitments pricing.

00:08:34.350 --> 00:08:38.070
This makes them the lowest
total cost of ownership, or TCO,

00:08:38.070 --> 00:08:40.500
offering available
in Google Cloud.

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:42.690
They are a great
choice for web servers,

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:46.680
small to medium databases,
dev and test environments,

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:49.530
and many applications that
don't have strict performance

00:08:49.530 --> 00:08:51.210
requirements.

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:53.280
They offer a compatible
performance baseline

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.388
to the current N1
VMs for those of you

00:08:55.388 --> 00:08:56.430
who have been using them.

00:09:00.870 --> 00:09:04.920
E2 VMs are available
with up to 16 vCPUs

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.650
and 128 gigabytes of memory.

00:09:07.650 --> 00:09:10.150
And they also support
flexible configurations

00:09:10.150 --> 00:09:12.310
through custom machine
types that enable you

00:09:12.310 --> 00:09:15.850
to rightsize your resources.

00:09:15.850 --> 00:09:17.650
Some constraints you
should be aware of

00:09:17.650 --> 00:09:20.500
is that E2 VMs run
on any available CPU

00:09:20.500 --> 00:09:24.410
in our infrastructure,
across Intel and AMD.

00:09:24.410 --> 00:09:27.860
They also run across older and
more recent CPU generations.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:30.870
You cannot select your CPU.

00:09:30.870 --> 00:09:33.270
These VMs do not
offer you the ability

00:09:33.270 --> 00:09:36.030
to attach local storage or GPUs.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:39.130
However, they benefit from
dynamic resource management,

00:09:39.130 --> 00:09:41.310
which is an efficient
scheduling mechanism that

00:09:41.310 --> 00:09:44.160
enables us to give you only
the resources you need,

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:46.680
and leverage like
migration to move you

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:48.660
to a server host
with more resources

00:09:48.660 --> 00:09:50.460
as your application
asks for more.

00:09:53.020 --> 00:09:56.950
This Google technology
brings you a lot of benefits,

00:09:56.950 --> 00:09:59.080
as long as you have a
flexible workload that

00:09:59.080 --> 00:10:02.130
can benefit from these
resource constraints.

00:10:04.770 --> 00:10:06.700
To assess the benefits
to your workload,

00:10:06.700 --> 00:10:08.650
you can look at the
potential performance

00:10:08.650 --> 00:10:11.590
per dollar improvements
shown on this slide.

00:10:11.590 --> 00:10:14.260
We're showing here
examples of some benchmarks

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:17.140
and how they leverage
the E2 cost savings.

00:10:17.140 --> 00:10:19.810
For example, you can
see approximately 30%

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:23.320
higher throughput per dollar
for webserving workloads.

00:10:23.320 --> 00:10:25.390
The same benefits are
also available when

00:10:25.390 --> 00:10:27.930
looking at benchmarks that
represent app and dev testing.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:37.810
For workloads that could
benefit from these improvements,

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:41.530
E2 also offers a large
list of upcoming features

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.240
that you can look forward to.

00:10:43.240 --> 00:10:46.480
We are continuing to expand
the capabilities of E2 VMs

00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:49.900
with new features that include
making them generally available

00:10:49.900 --> 00:10:55.780
in more GCP regions, making
them leverage AMD CPUs, as well

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:58.780
as Intel CPUs, in
more locations.

00:10:58.780 --> 00:11:00.400
And also for the
first time offering

00:11:00.400 --> 00:11:02.760
committed use discounts
for shared core

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:04.960
smaller VMs, so
that you can even

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:07.280
get more economic benefits.

00:11:07.280 --> 00:11:08.830
And finally, we're
continuing to look

00:11:08.830 --> 00:11:11.430
at new machine types
that would expand

00:11:11.430 --> 00:11:12.910
the available
configurations, too.

00:11:16.860 --> 00:11:21.220
Now let's move to
N2 and N2D VMs.

00:11:21.220 --> 00:11:23.830
These VMs are the best
fit for a majority

00:11:23.830 --> 00:11:25.750
of your general
purpose workloads,

00:11:25.750 --> 00:11:28.750
as they prioritize flexibility
and offer leading price

00:11:28.750 --> 00:11:30.430
performance.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:34.300
N2 and N2D VMs are a great
fit for your enterprise

00:11:34.300 --> 00:11:37.690
applications, databases,
and many of your web

00:11:37.690 --> 00:11:39.850
and app serving workloads.

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:44.160
They are also our most
flexible VM offering.

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:45.990
They give you a
wide feature set,

00:11:45.990 --> 00:11:49.230
enabling granular control of
your resources and coverage

00:11:49.230 --> 00:11:52.560
across capabilities that
range from sole tenancy

00:11:52.560 --> 00:11:56.005
to the flexibility to add and
remove storage at any point,

00:11:56.005 --> 00:11:56.505
for example.

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:03.480
General purpose
N2 and N2D VMs are

00:12:03.480 --> 00:12:06.720
designed to support a
variety of workloads

00:12:06.720 --> 00:12:09.990
by offering a perfect balance
of compute and memory resources

00:12:09.990 --> 00:12:15.420
with up to 200 vCPUs and
800 gigabytes of memory.

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:18.600
Flexibility is really a core
priority and a differentiator

00:12:18.600 --> 00:12:20.910
for this VM family.

00:12:20.910 --> 00:12:24.220
Our VMs, or virtual
machines, under N2 and N2D

00:12:24.220 --> 00:12:26.550
enable you to rightsize
your resources, which

00:12:26.550 --> 00:12:28.650
is a great cost-saving feature.

00:12:28.650 --> 00:12:32.010
They also enable you to auto
upgrade to new CPU generations

00:12:32.010 --> 00:12:35.650
as we launch the latest
architectures in the market.

00:12:35.650 --> 00:12:38.970
This means that you can simply
choose a minimum CPU platform,

00:12:38.970 --> 00:12:42.150
and Google will automatically
run your VM on the latest CPU

00:12:42.150 --> 00:12:44.670
generations.

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:46.800
You can attach up
to nine terabytes

00:12:46.800 --> 00:12:49.720
of local storage per node.

00:12:49.720 --> 00:12:51.840
And you can get the highest
networking performance

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:55.290
with 32 gigabits per second.

00:12:55.290 --> 00:12:57.650
These VMs are also being
rolled out globally

00:12:57.650 --> 00:13:02.760
across all of our Google
Compute Engine regions.

00:13:02.760 --> 00:13:05.700
Thinking about the key
differences between N2 and N2D

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:09.520
VMs, you can see an overview
summarized over here.

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:13.290
N2 VMs are our general
purpose Intel VMs,

00:13:13.290 --> 00:13:16.410
supporting the latest second
generation scalable processor

00:13:16.410 --> 00:13:18.940
from Intel with up to 80 vCPUs.

00:13:22.070 --> 00:13:26.330
They come with approximately 20
to 30 performance improvements,

00:13:26.330 --> 00:13:29.060
giving you a great price
performance benefit

00:13:29.060 --> 00:13:32.420
over the older generations.

00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:37.530
N2D are our AMD-based
general purpose VMs.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.740
They leverage the
latest Epyc Rome CPUs,

00:13:40.740 --> 00:13:45.060
and they come with up
to 224 vCPUs per node.

00:13:45.060 --> 00:13:46.740
They even offer
you a lower price

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:50.040
point, with approximately
13% lower pricing

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:51.255
versus comparable VMs.

00:13:55.760 --> 00:13:57.590
Now let's move to
some benchmarks

00:13:57.590 --> 00:14:00.500
and show you how you can benefit
from our latest general purpose

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.670
VMs.

00:14:02.670 --> 00:14:04.320
Over here, you can
see a wide range

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:06.270
of benchmarks
showing improvements

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:11.490
that range from 25% to 30%
for most workload types.

00:14:11.490 --> 00:14:16.070
You can see that N2 VMs
show great improvements when

00:14:16.070 --> 00:14:20.960
looking at benchmarks
like SAPBenchmarkDB,

00:14:20.960 --> 00:14:24.230
for example, which represents
high-end databases.

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:27.500
And over there we can see
30% higher SAPs compared

00:14:27.500 --> 00:14:29.780
to comparable VM types.

00:14:29.780 --> 00:14:32.480
When we look at the benchmarks
like Chromium Complie,

00:14:32.480 --> 00:14:35.480
you can see that you're
achieving approximately 25%

00:14:35.480 --> 00:14:37.350
improvement in little time--

00:14:37.350 --> 00:14:39.110
a great example of
the benefits that you

00:14:39.110 --> 00:14:44.350
can see with N2 VMs for
many of your applications.

00:14:44.350 --> 00:14:48.380
Now let's look at some
benchmarks for N2D.

00:14:48.380 --> 00:14:51.590
As you can see, there
are many benefits

00:14:51.590 --> 00:14:54.260
that we can see when looking
at data management workloads,

00:14:54.260 --> 00:14:56.660
given that these leverage
AMD's higher memory

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:59.700
bandwidth and higher
persistent throughput,

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:02.660
which is available with
the larger VM shapes.

00:15:02.660 --> 00:15:05.390
We've actually seen up to
2x performance improvements

00:15:05.390 --> 00:15:07.610
for some workloads,
like video streaming,

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:11.550
which are represented here
through the stream benchmark.

00:15:11.550 --> 00:15:13.200
And they show how
this higher memory

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:15.000
bandwidth can be
of great benefit

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:16.380
to many of your workloads.

00:15:21.860 --> 00:15:25.300
So in summary, N2 and N2D
VMs offer a wide range

00:15:25.300 --> 00:15:28.150
of capabilities and
also have a rich roadmap

00:15:28.150 --> 00:15:30.040
as we think about
the capabilities

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:32.860
that we're bringing
into these VM types.

00:15:32.860 --> 00:15:36.520
Features that we're working on
range from bare metal instances

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.110
to higher networking
performance,

00:15:38.110 --> 00:15:43.320
with up to 100
gigabytes per second.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:45.890
We're also working on
bringing you the latest CPU

00:15:45.890 --> 00:15:49.370
platforms across
both Intel and AMD,

00:15:49.370 --> 00:15:51.170
and you will see
those continuing

00:15:51.170 --> 00:15:53.548
to come very quickly to
these instance types.

00:15:58.430 --> 00:16:01.180
Now that we've covered the
general purpose VMs, let's move

00:16:01.180 --> 00:16:03.835
to some of our
workload-optimized VM families.

00:16:07.460 --> 00:16:09.470
We'll start with
compute-optimized VMs.

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:14.980
Performance-sensitive and
compute-bound workloads

00:16:14.980 --> 00:16:17.440
come in many shapes and forms.

00:16:17.440 --> 00:16:21.040
They might be high performance
interactive web servers,

00:16:21.040 --> 00:16:24.910
AAA games, high performance
computing across simulations,

00:16:24.910 --> 00:16:28.220
genomic analysis, or
media transcoding.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:29.830
They might also be
applications that

00:16:29.830 --> 00:16:32.230
have very expensive
per core licensing

00:16:32.230 --> 00:16:35.920
and thus would benefit from
higher per core performance.

00:16:35.920 --> 00:16:38.940
All of these applications
have one thing in common.

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:41.860
They would see great advantages
from leveraging the higher

00:16:41.860 --> 00:16:45.730
per core performance and the
fastest CPUs you can give them.

00:16:45.730 --> 00:16:47.670
And this is where
compute-optimized VMs

00:16:47.670 --> 00:16:49.800
can really supercharge
your applications.

00:16:54.040 --> 00:16:57.220
The compute-optimized VMs are
one of our latest additions

00:16:57.220 --> 00:17:01.050
to the Google Compute Engine
virtual machine portfolio.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:02.870
They offer the latest CPUs--

00:17:02.870 --> 00:17:06.579
Intel scalable
processors, Cascade Lake.

00:17:06.579 --> 00:17:09.130
They offer the highest
frequency per core

00:17:09.130 --> 00:17:11.740
available on Google
Compute Engine,

00:17:11.740 --> 00:17:15.420
with up to 3.8 gigahertz.

00:17:15.420 --> 00:17:19.560
And they offer multiple
shapes that go from four vCPUs

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:22.930
to 60 vCPUs.

00:17:22.930 --> 00:17:25.770
They also enable you to attach
local storage, if that's

00:17:25.770 --> 00:17:28.079
needed by your application,
and offer you up

00:17:28.079 --> 00:17:32.950
to 32 gigabits per second
networking bandwidth.

00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:35.720
With capability such as
NUMA-aware performance

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:37.460
and higher performance
per threat,

00:17:37.460 --> 00:17:42.350
as well as higher isolation for
latency sensitive workloads,

00:17:42.350 --> 00:17:48.290
these VMs can offer you a great
boost for many applications.

00:17:48.290 --> 00:17:50.300
Looking at how this
can benefit you,

00:17:50.300 --> 00:17:52.760
let's look at some of
the benchmarks over here.

00:17:52.760 --> 00:17:56.600
These benchmarks represent both
highly interactive applications

00:17:56.600 --> 00:17:59.420
and high performance
computing workloads.

00:17:59.420 --> 00:18:01.130
The results are evident.

00:18:01.130 --> 00:18:04.730
All of them see approximately
40% to 41% performance

00:18:04.730 --> 00:18:06.990
improvements.

00:18:06.990 --> 00:18:10.140
This can see over here in the
increase in the average queries

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:14.040
per second and also the
decrease in the average runtime

00:18:14.040 --> 00:18:15.270
seen under Open FOAM.

00:18:17.890 --> 00:18:19.660
With these benefits
in mind, we also

00:18:19.660 --> 00:18:22.000
wanted to share with you some
of the capabilities we're

00:18:22.000 --> 00:18:26.750
adding to the compute-optimized
VMs over the next few months.

00:18:26.750 --> 00:18:31.190
These include bare metal
instances support, as well as

00:18:31.190 --> 00:18:34.910
an increase in networking
performance with 100 gigabits

00:18:34.910 --> 00:18:37.570
per second coming soon.

00:18:37.570 --> 00:18:39.640
As we've mentioned for
the general purpose VMs,

00:18:39.640 --> 00:18:43.150
we're also always looking at
the latest CPU innovations

00:18:43.150 --> 00:18:45.250
so that we can always
offer you improving

00:18:45.250 --> 00:18:48.770
price performance as you utilize
some of these VM capabilities.

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:56.480
The second family under
our optimized VM types

00:18:56.480 --> 00:18:57.950
is memory-optimized VMs.

00:19:01.590 --> 00:19:04.680
Memory-optimized VMs are
designed for high-end business

00:19:04.680 --> 00:19:08.440
critical applications
and specifically SAP.

00:19:08.440 --> 00:19:10.830
They are a great fit
for in-memory databases

00:19:10.830 --> 00:19:13.740
like SAP HANA,
in-memory analytics,

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:15.450
and in-memory persistent cache.

00:19:19.140 --> 00:19:21.670
They offer you
the highest memory

00:19:21.670 --> 00:19:25.140
per core available on Google
Compute Engine and the lowest

00:19:25.140 --> 00:19:29.600
price per gig of memory as well.

00:19:29.600 --> 00:19:33.070
They feature the latest and
fastest memory technologies

00:19:33.070 --> 00:19:34.810
and come in
different shapes that

00:19:34.810 --> 00:19:38.830
range from one terabyte
of memory per node per VM,

00:19:38.830 --> 00:19:43.240
up to 12 terabytes per node,
which is really enough memory

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:47.940
to run your most
memory-intensive applications.

00:19:47.940 --> 00:19:51.450
In addition to the shapes and
capabilities of these VMs,

00:19:51.450 --> 00:19:55.940
they are also optimized for
flexibility and simplicity.

00:19:55.940 --> 00:19:58.730
For example, capabilities
like live migration

00:19:58.730 --> 00:20:01.040
helps you minimize
downtime, even

00:20:01.040 --> 00:20:03.490
as you run workloads
that require

00:20:03.490 --> 00:20:06.360
12 terabytes of data in memory.

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:08.090
We can take away a
lot of the overhead

00:20:08.090 --> 00:20:11.060
for operating those VMs.

00:20:11.060 --> 00:20:13.490
They also come with
flexible commitments

00:20:13.490 --> 00:20:15.950
across all the memory
optimized shapes,

00:20:15.950 --> 00:20:19.190
so that they can continue to
grow with you as your data

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:21.510
size grows.

00:20:21.510 --> 00:20:24.030
A great example of that
is illustrated over here

00:20:24.030 --> 00:20:25.530
when we look at
some of the customer

00:20:25.530 --> 00:20:29.910
case studies for our customers
who have been using those VMs

00:20:29.910 --> 00:20:33.250
and referencing one of the
testimonials we've seen.

00:20:33.250 --> 00:20:36.870
We've seen customers with
9 terabytes of SAP data

00:20:36.870 --> 00:20:40.840
that is growing approximately
at 1 terabyte per year.

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:42.720
So for them to be
able to leverage

00:20:42.720 --> 00:20:46.740
our flexible commitments and
the many sizes we offer simply

00:20:46.740 --> 00:20:50.130
means that they can maintain
support for their data size

00:20:50.130 --> 00:20:51.330
as it grows on our platform.

00:20:55.540 --> 00:20:59.340
If you have workloads that
would benefit from these memory

00:20:59.340 --> 00:21:02.550
configurations and would benefit
from the memory-optimized VMs,

00:21:02.550 --> 00:21:04.170
you also have a
lot of capabilities

00:21:04.170 --> 00:21:05.610
to look forward to.

00:21:05.610 --> 00:21:07.650
Some of the capabilities
that are coming soon

00:21:07.650 --> 00:21:11.460
to our memory-optimized VMs
include higher networking

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:13.890
and storage
performance, as well as

00:21:13.890 --> 00:21:16.380
additional SAP certifications.

00:21:16.380 --> 00:21:19.710
And of course, newer CPU
platforms and additional

00:21:19.710 --> 00:21:23.730
shapes so that you can
always rightsize your VM

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:25.770
to the requirements
of your applications

00:21:25.770 --> 00:21:28.540
and your databases.

00:21:28.540 --> 00:21:31.750
Now that we've given you a
quick overview of all of the VM

00:21:31.750 --> 00:21:35.680
families and virtual machine
capabilities available on GCP,

00:21:35.680 --> 00:21:38.500
we wanted to leave you with
a few statements on how

00:21:38.500 --> 00:21:42.660
to best get started with
these virtual machine types.

00:21:42.660 --> 00:21:44.600
So for your workloads
that would benefit

00:21:44.600 --> 00:21:46.760
from the memory-optimized
VMs, we also

00:21:46.760 --> 00:21:50.210
have a very rich roadmap
that you can look forward to.

00:21:50.210 --> 00:21:52.580
This includes networking
and storage performance

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:53.780
improvements.

00:21:53.780 --> 00:21:56.510
We're looking at 100
gig enhanced networking

00:21:56.510 --> 00:21:59.090
capabilities, as well
as new capabilities

00:21:59.090 --> 00:22:03.012
under extreme storage,
extreme persistent disk, that

00:22:03.012 --> 00:22:04.970
would bring you the
highest storage performance

00:22:04.970 --> 00:22:07.280
for these large VMs.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:10.550
We're also looking at
more SAP certifications

00:22:10.550 --> 00:22:12.320
so that you can
run more workloads

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:14.580
on these virtual machines.

00:22:14.580 --> 00:22:18.830
And as always, we're looking at
newer CPU platforms, as well as

00:22:18.830 --> 00:22:22.490
additional shapes, so that you
can always rightsize your VM

00:22:22.490 --> 00:22:25.460
and leverage only the
resources that you truly need,

00:22:25.460 --> 00:22:28.440
minimizing waste.

00:22:28.440 --> 00:22:31.800
Now that we've taken you
through all of our VM types

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:33.660
and families, we
wanted to leave you

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.730
with a quick guide
for how to get started

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:38.310
with these different VM
types and these different VM

00:22:38.310 --> 00:22:39.570
families.

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:42.570
We look at this as a
simple three-step process.

00:22:42.570 --> 00:22:45.600
For the first step, consider
your resource specs.

00:22:45.600 --> 00:22:47.370
What are the shapes
and resources

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:49.470
needed by your applications?

00:22:49.470 --> 00:22:53.610
How sensitive are you to
specific CPUs architectures?

00:22:53.610 --> 00:22:57.570
You, for example, need to use
something like Intel AVX 512,

00:22:57.570 --> 00:22:59.940
and need to make sure that
you're running on CPUs

00:22:59.940 --> 00:23:02.490
that have this capability.

00:23:02.490 --> 00:23:05.640
Do you need to qualify
every new CPU architecture,

00:23:05.640 --> 00:23:07.530
or are you more
flexible in terms

00:23:07.530 --> 00:23:10.270
of the underlying hardware?

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:12.545
After you've thought
about that, we

00:23:12.545 --> 00:23:14.170
would recommend that
you start thinking

00:23:14.170 --> 00:23:16.670
about price performance.

00:23:16.670 --> 00:23:18.340
Which of your
applications would benefit

00:23:18.340 --> 00:23:20.090
from higher performance?

00:23:20.090 --> 00:23:22.990
Which of your applications
are highly interactive when

00:23:22.990 --> 00:23:25.570
you need the shortest
runtime and the best

00:23:25.570 --> 00:23:28.220
latency for example?

00:23:28.220 --> 00:23:31.340
That would be one
category of applications

00:23:31.340 --> 00:23:34.760
that would benefit from our
highest-performant compute

00:23:34.760 --> 00:23:37.770
instances, like
compute-optimize VMs.

00:23:37.770 --> 00:23:41.020
On the other side, what
are the applications

00:23:41.020 --> 00:23:44.380
that would be a great fit
for additional cost savings?

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:47.620
Over there, we'd recommend
that you look at VM types

00:23:47.620 --> 00:23:50.050
like E2 VMs, for example,
that can give you

00:23:50.050 --> 00:23:52.560
a lot of cost savings.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:55.550
So once you've had a chance
to look at your resource specs

00:23:55.550 --> 00:23:58.490
and look at your pricing
and performance priorities,

00:23:58.490 --> 00:24:01.770
now it's time to think
about deployment planning.

00:24:01.770 --> 00:24:03.510
As you think about
your deployment plan,

00:24:03.510 --> 00:24:05.718
we would recommend you start
looking at your capacity

00:24:05.718 --> 00:24:07.920
requirements and
looking at the regions

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.350
where you want to
be running your VMs.

00:24:10.350 --> 00:24:13.890
You can also find a lot of
resources on our Google Compute

00:24:13.890 --> 00:24:16.480
Engine documentation
pages that can guide you

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:18.940
through the migration process.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:22.170
This concludes our overview
of the Google Compute Engine

00:24:22.170 --> 00:24:24.810
virtual machine types
introduction today.

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:26.640
We hope that it was
useful and that you

00:24:26.640 --> 00:24:29.590
have a lot of information
to get started with.

00:24:29.590 --> 00:24:31.480
Thank you so much for
being with us today.

00:24:31.480 --> 00:24:33.440
That's all for today.

